 All dynamic operations passing nodes into sysctl require that the
 version be explicitly specified.
 .Pp
+A node's name may be up to
+.Dv SYSCTL_MAXNAMELEN
+bytes long, but
+.Fa sysctl_name
+holds only the first
+.Dv SYSCTL_NAMELEN
+\- 1 of them.
+With
+.Dv SYSCTL_VERS_2
+in
+.Fa sysctl_flags ,
+nodes are passed in and out instead as
+.Vt struct sysctlrec
+records, each the node followed by the whole name, which
+.Fn NEXT_SYSCTLREC
+steps through.
+The node in a record carries none of the kernel's pointers:
+.Fa sysctl_func ,
+.Fa sysctl_parent ,
+.Fa sysctl_desc
+and the child or data pointer are
+.Dv NULL :
+.Pp
+.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
+struct sysctlrec {
+        struct sysctlnode rec_node;    /* sysctl_name cut short */
+        uint32_t rec_len;              /* bytes in this record */
+        uint32_t rec_namelen;          /* bytes of the name, with nul */
+        char rec_name[8];              /* the name, really rec_namelen */
+};
+.Ed
+.Pp
+The
+.Fn asysctlquery
+function returns the children of the node at
+.Fa name
+as such records, in memory allocated with
+.Xr malloc 3 ,
+with
+.Fa len
+set to its size.
+On a kernel that does not know
+.Dv SYSCTL_VERS_2
+the records are made from the nodes, with the names they have.
+.Pp
 Creation and destruction of nodes works by constructing part of a new
 node description (or a description of the existing node) and invoking
 .Dv CTL_CREATE
